// 035 — Type aliases — DESIGN NOTE
// `typedef PlayerId = Integer` or `type Meters = f64` lets you name a
// semantic unit that's structurally just another base type. The
// readability gain is real; the safety gain depends entirely on
// whether the alias is nominal (PlayerId ≠ Integer) or structural
// (they're interchangeable).
//
// AiLang has no alias mechanism right now. In practice, two patterns
// cover the readability use-case:
//
//   1. Naming convention on the variable side. `player_id`, `score`,
//      `timestamp_ns` — the meaning travels with the identifier.
//      No type-checker help, but no false confidence either.
//
//   2. FixedPool field names. When a bundle of Integers has semantics
//      (PlayerState.hp, PlayerState.xp, PlayerState.level), the pool
//      field names carry the same meaning a type alias would, and
//      you get named access for free. See 094_multi_return_via_pool.
//
// A nominal type alias would prevent `multiply_player_by_seconds` —
// nothing AiLang does today stops you. The trade is a thinner type
// system vs. one less surface for mistakes per compile error.
SubRoutine.Main {
    player_id = 42
    score = 1200
    timestamp_ns = 1714530000000000

    PrintMessage("player_id    = ")
    PrintNumber(player_id)
    PrintMessage("\nscore        = ")
    PrintNumber(score)
    PrintMessage("\ntimestamp_ns = ")
    PrintNumber(timestamp_ns)
    PrintMessage("\n")
}
RunTask(Main)
